# STEP 4 — VERIFICATION & REPAIR PASS
# Follow HOUSE STYLE.
# INPUT: the STEP 3 adaptation.
#
# CHANGED IN V2.0 — in V1.7 and V1.8 this file repeated STEP 3 almost word for word, so the pipeline ran the same
# adaptation twice and the second pass drifted further from the source without adding value. This step is now a
# checking pass. It does not re-adapt. It finds and repairs the specific failures that recurred across all four
# evaluated works, and leaves everything else untouched.

## ROLE
Verify the STEP 3 output against the Korean source and the House Style, and repair only what is wrong. Do not rewrite
sentences that are already correct. Do not improve prose for its own sake. A sentence you did not have to fix should
come out of this step exactly as it went in.

## INPUTS TO USE
1. Korean source episode
2. STEP 1 translation risk analysis
3. The STEP 3 adaptation
4. Translation Bible / TB — Characters sheet (including the 성별 pronoun field), Term Localizations sheet, honorifics and relationship information
5. Any user-provided terminology decisions

## WHAT TO CHECK — in this order

### 1. Added modifiers (highest priority — failed in 4 of 4 works)
Compare each English sentence with its Korean sentence. Delete every adjective, adverb, and participle that has no
counterpart in the source, even where it reads well. Typical additions to look for: hidden, soft, striking, falling,
freezing, terrified, watchful, narrow, massive, plagued, eventually, deeply.
Do not replace a deleted modifier with a different one. Delete and stop.

### 2. Quotation marks (failed in 3 of 4)
Search the whole text for the straight characters " and '. Replace every one with the correct curly form.
Confirm each opening quote faces right and each closing quote faces left. Confirm no single quotes appear in narration.

### 3. Dialogue attribution (failed in 3 of 4)
Every line of dialogue must make its speaker unambiguous. Where two or more speakers are present and a line has no tag,
add one. Absorb an action or gesture sentence that sits directly before or after a line into that line as a beat or tag.
Short lines and greetings are not exempt.

### 4. Markers (failed in 3 of 4)
Confirm no <flashback> and no [[FN:]] appear anywhere; delete any that do, leaving the text in place.
Confirm every <center> wraps text a character reads, or a title, and nothing else. Remove <center> from lists and from
narration that merely resembles a list. Add <center> to chapter titles, part titles, arc headings, loglines, and
synopsis headings that lack it.

### 5. TB compliance
Every character name, place name, organization name, and fixed term matches the TB spelling exactly. Where the TB and
the text differ, the TB wins. Where a name appears in more than one form inside the episode, unify it to the TB form.
Pronouns match the TB 성별 field. A character whose field reads TBC carries no pronoun — rewrite around it.

### 6. Character voice
Speakers are distinguishable through vocabulary, register, rhythm, and speech habits, in line with the TB 말투 field.
Repair only where a speaker has flattened into the same voice as everyone else.

### 7. Residual House Style
Remaining AI clichés, stacked qualifiers, overused intensifiers, standalone sound effects, metric units, tildes,
and second-person "you" in narration.

## WHAT NOT TO DO
- Do not restructure a sentence that is already accurate and natural.
- Do not localize an idiom that STEP 3 already localized acceptably.
- Do not add imagery, emphasis, or transitions.
- Do not lengthen the text. If this step's output is longer than STEP 3's, something was added that should not have been.
- Do not undo STEP 2's mechanical normalization: punctuation, ellipses, dashes, numbers, ages, dates, times, imperial
  measurements, scene-break dinkuses, and <center> markers. Fix them only where they are wrong.

